title: A plaintext emit writes a non-finite float's contradictory string half verbatim
updated: 2026-08-21T07:06:29Z
---

Found while measuring karr #134 / docs/adr/0037 (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops).
Pre-existing, unmoved by that change, and deliberately left alone there.

A non-finite float that carries a PUBLIC string half is written by the
plaintext emitters exactly as that half reads, whatever it says:

  Format::YAML->emit({v => dualvar(+Inf, "banana")})  ->  v: banana
  Format::JSON->emit({v => dualvar(+Inf, "banana")})  ->  {"v":"banana"}
  the same for ".INf", ".infinity", "Inf", and for the contradictions
  dualvar(+Inf,"-.inf") / dualvar(-Inf,".inf") / dualvar(NaN,".inf")

The MAC-covered paths refuse every one of those already: assert_representable
gates on _carries_go_non_finite_token, and ADR 0013 guard has the last word.
Only a plaintext emit -- decrypt_file, edit, or a direct emit() call -- writes
them, and the leaf then reparses as the STRING it says, so a decrypt_file ->
encrypt_file round trip retypes it.

Not fixed in ADR 0037 on purpose: repairing it means overwriting a string half
the caller chose, on the strength of the number beside it, which is exactly the
guess ADR 0012 refuses to make for an integer whose halves disagree. ADR 0037
narrowed its own repair to a leaf with NO public PV for that reason.

Reachable only by a caller who constructs such a dualvar by hand -- no parse
and no decryption produces one -- which is why this is medium rather than high.
Wire lane. Decide whether the plaintext emitters should refuse it (naming the
key path, the way the MAC-covered paths do) rather than writing a document
their own reader will read differently.
